e><source>Br J Gen Pract</source><year>2000</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>559</fpage><lpage>563</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10954938</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Effects of an appropriate medical care in the emergency department on prognosis. Effects of an appropriate (full bars) or inappropriate (hatched bars) diagnosis in the emergency department <bold>(a) </bold>or initial emergency treatment <bold>(b) </bold>on the number of hospital-free days within 1 month after admission (expressed as median), percentage of patients admitted to intensive care unit (ICU), or mortality. NS, not significant.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cc4926-1"></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Kaplan-Meier estimates of survival according to the initial treatment received in the emergency department. Inappropriate treatment was noted in 162 (32%) of the 514 patients. The log-rank test was used to calculate <italic>p</italic>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cc4926-2"></graphic></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Mortality (%) according to the five variables (X axis) associated with death in the multivariate analysis.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="cc4926-3"></graphic></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Patient characteristics</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Variable</td><td align="center">Men (<italic>n </italic>= 253)</td><td align="center">Women (<italic>n </italic>= 261)</td><td align="center">All patients (<italic>n </italic>= 514)</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Age (years)</td><td align="center">78 &#x000b1; 8</td><td align="center">82 &#x000b1; 9<sup>a</sup></td><td align="center">80 &#x000b1; 9</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Age &#x02265; 70 years</td><td 